menu "e32p: twai"

    config E32P_TWAI_ENABLE
        bool "Enable e32p_twai (CAN 2.0 / TWAI)"
        depends on SOC_TWAI_SUPPORTED
        default n
        help
            Facade over the ESP-IDF TWAI (CAN 2.0) controller: install, start,
            transmit and receive frames. A real bus needs an external transceiver;
            NO_ACK mode + a self frame allows a jumper-only loopback test.

    config E32P_TWAI_RX_QUEUE_LEN
        int "TWAI driver RX queue depth (frames)"
        depends on E32P_TWAI_ENABLE
        range 5 64
        default 5
        help
            How many received frames the driver buffers before dropping. The
            IDF default is 5, which is ~2.5 ms of wire time at 250 kbit but only
            ~0.6 ms at 1 Mbit - too little if a higher-priority task preempts
            mid-burst. Raise it for high bitrates or bursty multi-frame
            transports such as ISO-TP. Costs sizeof(twai_message_t) (~16 B) per
            slot.

    config E32P_TWAI_TX_QUEUE_LEN
        int "TWAI driver TX queue depth (frames)"
        depends on E32P_TWAI_ENABLE
        range 5 64
        default 5
        help
            How many frames may be queued for transmission. This bounds burst
            throughput: a sender that refills the queue once per poll tick moves
            at most this many frames per tick, so a shallow queue caps a
            segmented transport regardless of bitrate. Raise it alongside the RX
            depth when running ISO-TP at 500 kbit or 1 Mbit.

            NOTE: a deeper TX queue means more frames may still be in flight
            when you stop the controller. Drain with twai_get_status_info()
            .msgs_to_tx before twai_stop(), or the tail of a transfer is aborted.

endmenu
